  "priority": 2,
  "description": "Astronomical tithi boundary instants across one synodic month. The BOUNDARY only - which civil day a tithi is credited to is the udaya rule, which is cultural and lives in the calendar-rule layer.",
  "source": {
    "authority": "Swiss Ephemeris 2.10.03 (DE441) evaluated against a mathematical definition",
    "kind": "mathematical_definition",
    "definition": "A tithi boundary is the instant at which (Moon longitude - Sun longitude) mod 360 reaches an exact multiple of 12 deg. Tithi number = floor(elongation / 12) + 1, giving 30 tithis per synodic month.",
    "method": "Independent bisection in tests/golden/definitions.py on swe.calc_ut(MOON) - swe.calc_ut(SUN), bracketed over 2 days (a tithi averages ~0.98 days), converged to 1 second. Elongation is monotonic across the bracket, so bisection is safe.",
    "reference": "tests/golden/definitions.py::tithi_boundary_jd",
    "publication_year": "n/a (derived, not published)",
    "convention": "Elongation of APPARENT geocentric longitudes. Ayanamsha-INDEPENDENT: it is a difference of two longitudes, so the ayanamsha cancels - this is why no ayanamsha field appears here, unlike sankranti. UT timescale. 32 consecutive boundaries starting 2026-06-10, covering a full synodic month plus overlap.",
    "retrieved": "2026-08-02",
    "verified_by": "Convergence checked: solved elongations land within 4e-5 deg of the target multiple of 12 deg. Boundary spacing averages ~0.98 days, matching the synodic month / 30.",
    "notes": "Kshaya and adhika tithi are CONSEQUENCES of these boundaries falling either side of sunrise, not separate astronomical events. They belong to the calendar-rule layer and are covered by the byte-identical harness, not here."
  },
  "tolerance": {
    "value": 60,
    "unit": "seconds",
    "rationale": "Production's find_tithi_end defaults to tolerance_seconds=60 and the reference converges to 1 s, so 60 s accepts production's stated precision. Near a boundary the Moon moves ~0.5 arcmin/minute, so 60 s corresponds to ~0.5 arcmin of elongation - far below the 12 deg tithi span, yet tight enough that a convergence fault (which moves the answer by hours) is caught."
  },
  "reconciled_under_provenance": "1a29d9327c09017b650e42c91ae90f44da6f213e084fd4518f849be901a80ec8",
  "required_entry_fields": [
